---
// Course Overview section (Task 3, Course PDP Chunk A) — Figma 675:4354
// (desktop, ≥1024, file FX7PDNvhZwyozODaq8Q8i7) / 1109:14378 (mobile — the
// "Sole Switch Pro Page" > "Course Overview" instance inside the
// professional-courses mobile frame 1109:14374). Prop is `{ item }` (same
// shape as CourseDetails.astro), reading `item.pdp.overview`.
//
// `item.pdp.overview` = `{ image, body, details: [{ label, value }] }`
// (this task's brief-mandated shape). Mapping that onto the frame's actual
// content: the 5 short label/value pairs (Course Length, Evidence Based,
// Course Structure, Continuing Education Credit, and — despite its longer
// sentence-length value — Audience) are `details`; the closing "Course
// Concepts" paragraph is `body`, since it's the one piece of free-running
// descriptive copy rather than a terse label/value fact. Both frames put a
// teal "Course Concepts" LABEL above that paragraph (styled like a detail
// label); it renders here as static markup (like the "Course Overview"
// heading) since the brief's data shape has no field for the body's label
// and the text is verbatim on both frames. The "Course Overview" heading
// likewise isn't part of the data shape (no `heading` field in the brief's
// spec) — static markup here, verbatim on both frames.
//
// Desktop (675:4354) and mobile (1109:14378) DISAGREE on the details set,
// not just wording: desktop's 3rd/4th items read "Course Structure" /
// "Continuing Education Credit" ("Online on demand" / "Yes"); mobile's
// equivalent slots read "Course Format" / "Course Style" ("Online on
// demand" / "Video lecture") — a genuinely different 4th fact, not a label
// rename. Mobile's Audience value also differs from desktop's ("Anyone
// looking to understand how to choose healthy shoes for themselves" vs.
// "Healthcare professionals looking to improve their footwear
// recommendations for clients and patients."). Since `details` is a single
// array with no separate desktop/mobile slot, desktop 675:4354 is kept as
// the one canonical source — same "pull desktop first" precedent
// CourseDetails.astro's `description` field set — flagged here rather than
// silently merged or guessed. See catalog.js's `overview` comment for the
// Figma-verbatim desktop values actually used.
//
// Layout: markup order matches mobile's literal visual order (heading ->
// details grid -> image -> body paragraph, per 1109:14378's screenshot).
// Desktop instead runs the image full-height alongside a right column
// (heading/details/body stacked in their existing DOM order) — reproduced
// with an explicit CSS Grid placement (`grid-row: 1 / -1` on the media box)
// rather than duplicating markup per breakpoint, since the two frames
// genuinely interleave the image at different points in the content flow
// (mid-stack on mobile, full side panel on desktop) — a plain flex reorder
// can't express that.
//
// Detail grid gap is a literal --space-5 (24px) at BOTH breakpoints — Figma
// measures the same 24px gap for every row/column split in both frames. The
// internal label-to-value gap inside each detail item does genuinely differ
// though: --space-2 (8px) on mobile vs --space-4 (16px) on desktop, per each
// frame's own measured value.
//
// Detail labels use --type-label (600/16px) + --color-teal — an exact match
// for mobile's own token binding (var(--text-label)). Desktop's pulled code
// shows an unbound literal Bold(700)/16px instead, but every other section
// in this codebase (CourseDetails, FourColumn, etc.) keeps a single type
// token consistent across both breakpoints rather than introducing a
// one-off desktop-only weight, so --type-label is used here too.
//
// Detail values, Audience, and the Course Concepts body all use
// --color-ink. Desktop's pulled code binds the short detail values to the
// ink token but renders Audience/Course Concepts as an unbound literal
// "black" instead — read as a Figma authoring inconsistency (ink #231F20 vs
// true #000 is imperceptible, and no other component here introduces a raw
// black), so ink is used uniformly.
//
// No dedicated course-overview photography exists (this course has never
// had a PDP before) — `image` reuses the same PLP course shot every other
// slot on this page already reuses (see catalog.js's `features` comment).
// Desktop's frame renders its image via an absolutely-positioned crop
// (213.88%/-90.73% offsets) — that's just Figma's internal expression of an
// object-fit:cover crop inside a flex-1/self-stretch panel, reproduced here
// as a plain overflow:hidden + object-fit:cover box (same idiom
// ProductDetails' gallery and FourColumn's card media already use), not the
// literal percentages. Mobile's image is a simple aspect-ratio (320/358)
// box using the same technique.
//
// Heading size: mobile's pulled code shows text-h3 resolving to 24px vs
// desktop's 32px. This repo's tokens.css has one non-responsive --type-h3
// (32px) and every sibling course section (CourseDetails, FourColumn) uses
// it unmodified at both breakpoints — same here, no mobile override added.
//
// ---- `body` as list OR paragraph (Chunk B2 review fix wave) --------------
// Trainer Certification's own "Course Concepts" block (683:10089 node
// `I683:10089;174:1437` desktop / 1116:17400 node `I1116:17400;181:2228`
// mobile) is a REAL 4-item bulleted `<ul>`, not free-running prose — unlike
// Sole Switch Pro's own paragraph, which is what this field was originally
// modeled on (see this file's own comment above). A prior task correctly
// refused to flatten Figma's actual list structure into a run-on sentence
// and left `body` unset (rendering nothing) rather than misrepresent it.
//
// Fixed here by letting `body` express EITHER shape, not by adding a new,
// parallel field name (e.g. a sibling `concepts` array): `Array.isArray
// (overview.body)` renders a `<ul>`; a plain (non-array) truthy value
// renders exactly the same lone `<p>` as before. This reuses the "array of
// plain strings = bullet list" shape already established elsewhere on this
// same PDP composer (`ImageWithText.astro`'s own `items`, `ThreeColumnInfo
// .astro`'s per-column `items`) rather than inventing a bespoke tuple, and
// deliberately does NOT reach for `set:html` the way PdpAccordion.astro's
// generic `content` field does — this branch already reverted a `set:html`
// sink once (ComparisonChart.astro's own intro paragraph) specifically
// because plain structured data was cleaner and safer than raw HTML for a
// field with no genuine embedded markup need; a 4-item array of strings has
// exactly the same "no real markup, just multiple lines" shape that fix
// argued for, so the same structured-data-over-HTML choice is made again
// here rather than re-introducing that pattern.
//
// Every course before Trainer Certification passes a plain string for
// `body` (or omits it) — `Array.isArray` is `false` for both, so `hasBody`/
// the render branch below are byte-identical to the pre-fix behaviour for
// all of them; this is a no-op except for Trainer Certification's own new
// array value.
//
// Ordered-list fix (Chunk B2 review wave): FGA Level 2's "Course Concepts"
// list is a decimal ordered list in Figma, NOT bullets like every other
// course. Polymorphic `body` already handles array vs. string; now
// `overview.ordered: true` renders `<ol>` instead of `<ul>` for that
// course's array. Absent `ordered` defaults to `<ul>` — unchanged for all
// pre-existing courses (no `ordered` set).
//
// Guard fix (final review fix wave): `details`/`image` used to render
// unconditionally — `<img src={overview.image}>` with no guard gave a
// broken-image box for a course whose `overview` omits `image`, and a bare
// `overview.details?.map(...)` still emitted an empty
// `.course-overview__details` grid `<div>` for one that omits `details`.
// Both are now wrapped the same way `hasBody`'s block already was: a real
// element only when there's real content (`details.length > 0` /
// `Boolean(image)`). Every shipped course sets both fields, so this is a
// byte-identical no-op today — the Pdp.astro registry entry for this
// section was ALSO fixed in the same pass so `overview: {}` (or one with
// only empty fields) can no longer reach this component at all; this
// guard is the second layer, not the only one.
const { item } = Astro.props;
const overview = item.pdp?.overview;
const bodyIsList = Array.isArray(overview?.body);
const bodyIsOrdered = bodyIsList && overview?.ordered === true;
const hasBody = bodyIsList ? overview.body.length > 0 : Boolean(overview?.body);
---
{overview && (
  <section class="course-overview" data-pdp-id={item.id}>
    <div class="course-overview__inner">
      <h2 class="course-overview__heading">Course Overview</h2>

      {overview.details?.length > 0 && (
        <div class="course-overview__details">
          {overview.details.map((detail) => (
            <div class="course-overview__detail">
              <p class="course-overview__detail-label">{detail.label}</p>
              <p class="course-overview__detail-value">{detail.value}</p>
            </div>
          ))}
        </div>
      )}

      {overview.image && (
        <div class="course-overview__media">
          <img src={overview.image} alt="" loading="lazy" />
        </div>
      )}

      {hasBody && (
        <div class="course-overview__concepts">
          <p class="course-overview__detail-label">Course Concepts</p>
          {bodyIsList ? (
            bodyIsOrdered ? (
              <ol class="course-overview__list course-overview__list--ordered">
                {overview.body.map((text) => <li>{text}</li>)}
              </ol>
            ) : (
              <ul class="course-overview__list">
                {overview.body.map((text) => <li>{text}</li>)}
              </ul>
            )
          ) : (
            <p class="course-overview__body">{overview.body}</p>
          )}
        </div>
      )}
    </div>
  </section>
)}
<style>
  .course-overview__inner {
    max-width: var(--container-max);
    margin-inline: auto;
    padding-inline: var(--space-5);
    padding-block: var(--space-7);
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: var(--space-5);
  }

  .course-overview__heading {
    font: var(--type-h3);
    color: var(--color-ink);
  }

  .course-overview__details {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(2, 1fr);
    gap: var(--space-5);
  }
  /* Audience is always the last detail — its value is a full sentence, not
     a terse fact, and both frames give it the full row width. */
  .course-overview__detail:last-child {
    grid-column: 1 / -1;
  }
  .course-overview__detail {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: var(--space-2);
  }
  .course-overview__detail-label {
    font: var(--type-label);
    color: var(--color-teal);
  }
  .course-overview__detail-value {
    font: var(--type-body-sm);
    color: var(--color-ink);
  }

  .course-overview__media {
    width: 100%;
    aspect-ratio: 320 / 358;
    overflow: hidden;
    background: var(--color-border);
  }
  .course-overview__media img {
    width: 100%;
    height: 100%;
    object-fit: cover;
  }

  /* "Course Concepts" is a teal label + long-form paragraph — same
     label/value shape as a detail item (8px gap mobile / 16px desktop),
     reproducing the labeled closing block both frames show. The label text
     is static (verbatim on both frames), like the "Course Overview" heading. */
  .course-overview__concepts {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: var(--space-2);
  }
  .course-overview__body {
    font: var(--type-body-sm);
    color: var(--color-ink);
  }
  /* List variant of `body` (review fix wave — Trainer Certification's own
     "Course Concepts" 4-item `<ul>`, 683:10089;174:1439 / 1116:17400's
     equivalent node). Same font/colour as the paragraph variant above
     (Figma's own list uses the identical --text-body-sm/ink binding); 8px
     per-item gap (`mb-[8px]`, an exact --space-2 match on both frames) and
     21px bullet indent (`ms-[21px]`, the same literal-with-no-exact-token
     ImageWithText.astro's own desktop list already flags — 16/24 bracket it
     on this repo's --space scale) are both real Figma measurements, not
     invented. */
  .course-overview__list {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    padding-inline-start: 21px;
    margin: 0;
    list-style: disc;
  }
  .course-overview__list li {
    font: var(--type-body-sm);
    color: var(--color-ink);
    margin-bottom: var(--space-2);
  }
  .course-overview__list li:last-child {
    margin-bottom: 0;
  }
  /* Ordered variant of `body` (review fix wave — FGA Level 2's decimal
     "Course Concepts" `<ol>`, 1041:10413;174:1439 / 1116:16468's
     equivalent node). Rendering decimal list markers instead of bullets,
     while reusing every other `.course-overview__list` style (spacing,
     type, colour, indent). */
  .course-overview__list--ordered {
    list-style: decimal;
  }

  /* ---- Desktop (Figma 675:4354, ≥1024) -------------------------------- */
  @media (min-width: 1024px) {
    .course-overview__inner {
      display: grid;
      /* 651px content column measured off the 1200-wide frame (48 pad each
         side + 48 gap + 651 = 1200 - image's own flexible width) — same
         "literal px content column" idiom CourseDetails' 484px buybox
         uses. */
      grid-template-columns: 1fr 651px;
      /* 48px between image and content column (Figma's frame gap), but the
         internal content rows (heading -> details -> concepts) are a uniform
         24px per the frame — hence the split, not a single --space-7. */
      column-gap: var(--space-7);
      row-gap: var(--space-5);
      padding-inline: var(--space-7);
    }
    .course-overview__heading {
      grid-column: 2;
      grid-row: 1;
    }
    .course-overview__details {
      grid-column: 2;
      grid-row: 2;
      grid-template-columns: repeat(3, 1fr);
    }
    .course-overview__detail {
      gap: var(--space-4);
    }
    .course-overview__detail:last-child {
      grid-column: span 2;
    }
    .course-overview__media {
      grid-column: 1;
      grid-row: 1 / -1;
      align-self: stretch;
      height: 100%;
      aspect-ratio: auto;
    }
    .course-overview__concepts {
      grid-column: 2;
      grid-row: 3;
      gap: var(--space-4);
    }
  }
</style>
